 {"id":"CF-050","title":"Public repo publishes absolute host paths and internal worktree names: 27 tracked files, 102 hits, no publish gate","status":"pending","priority":"P3","complexity":2,"reasoning":"Measured on a fresh clone of origin/main e1e4bed274f8654324a6779ae6d131aad4db0339 with a clean working tree. git grep -lIE '/home/[a-z][a-z0-9_-]*/' HEAD names 27 tracked files with 102 hits: 17 under .gitreins/history/ (verdict.json, commit.patch and summary.md written by every judge run, recording absolute guard_log paths such as the host scratch root and ~/proj-wt-code-factory-<lane>-<date> worktree names), 8 under docs/audits/ (committed audit logs and .probe.txt transcripts), and 2 board files. Excluding .gitreins the same grep returns 45 hits across 10 files. .gitignore covers .gitreins/logs/, .gitreins/verdicts/, .gitreins/tasks.yaml and .gitreins/usage.jsonl but not .gitreins/history/, so the routine chore(gitreins) commits (for example 4638e90f) publish a growing corpus of host layout to a public repository. Nothing gates it: tests/selftest.sh item 6 checks literal credential assignments only, and the string 'absolute' does not appear in that file. Severity is layout exposure, not secret leakage: a credential-shape scan (ghp_, github_pat_, sk-, AKIA, xoxb-, PRIVATE KEY) returns 0 hits at the same commit, and GitHub secret scanning plus push protection are enabled. The related but distinct CF-045 covers dead absolute paths inside board closure evidence; this row covers what the published tree exposes to strangers.","acceptance_criteria":"1. A publish policy exists in the repository (docs/PUBLISH.md or a section of AGENTS.md) stating whether .gitreins/history/ is published, and if it is, that absolute host paths are replaced with ~/ or placeholders before commit, and naming the command used to check. 2. Authored documentation is clean: git grep -nIE '/home/[a-z][a-z0-9_-]*/' HEAD -- . ':(exclude).gitreins' ':(exclude).coding-hermes/board' returns no lines, or