 {"id":"CF-140","title":"P3 — clone_lane.py mints a lane with the source lane's cooldown and pin, and disables a live lane on re-run","status":"pending","priority":"P3","complexity":3,"reasoning":"Measured by the code-factory-qa tick code-factory-qa-2026-11-04-00-07-40 at origin/main d586336, in a detached worktree (git worktree add --detach <scratch> origin/main), probe executed from the repo root.\n\nscripts/clone_lane.py is the shipped tool that mints a lane row by cloning a canonical sibling (docstring: the scheduler's lane-onboarding gate refuses every workdir for docs/readme/perf/review families, so cloning a live row is the only way to give a new project the same shape). It reads the source row with SELECT * and copies the WHOLE row (`row = dict(src)`), overriding only name, workdir, namespace_id, parent, enabled=0, repo_url, prompt_mode, the three last_tick_* fields, consecutive_failures, created_at and updated_at. Everything else is inherited verbatim: cooldown_s, cooldown_pin_s, cooldown_pin_by, cooldown_pin_at, the bump_* set, board_rows_seen/board_open_seen and the qa/pm/sync/dogfood zero-output counters.\n\nThat inheritance is not theoretical: 868 of the 876 rows in ~/.hermes/coding-hermes/scheduler.db carry cooldown_pin_s > 0 today (values include 604800 = 7d, 21600 = 6h), so a clone of almost any live lane starts its life pinned. The cloned lane is written enabled=0, so the pin is invisible until an operator enables it — and enabling does not clear cooldown_pin_s, so the new lane's first tick is up to seven days away with no output and no error. That is the same user-visible outcome CF-134 (P1) describes, reached through a different mechanism.\n\nTwo more defects in the same write path, both reproduced by the probe: (1) when the destination name already exists the tool prints \"already exists — configuring it instead of creating\" and then UPDATEs every cloned column, including enabled=0, so re-running it to re-point a live lane silently DISABLES that